#!/usr/bin/python3 -I
"""The phone as the TV's remote: a page with the remote's buttons, the home screen's apps, the
TV's power, and a voice assistant (PiPA). Phones on the Pi's own Wi-Fi, the home network or the
tailnet open http://<pi>:8096/.

  GET  /                   the page
  GET  /apps               the home screen's apps: [{"id", "name", "logo"}]
  GET  /logo/<file>        an app's logo, from the menu's media folder
  POST /button/<name>      up, down, left, right, ok, back, home, close or keyboard: the same
                           as the TV remote's button (through cec-remote's socket)
  POST /tv/on, /tv/standby turn the TV on to the Pi's input, or off (kiosk wake / standby)
  POST /app/<id>           open an app on the TV (kiosk run <id>)
  GET  /ws                 WebSocket for the pointer: {"m": [dx, dy]}, {"c": "left"|"right"},
                           {"s": n}, passed on to cec-remote's virtual mouse
  GET  /assistant          {"name", "available"}: whether the voice assistant answers
  POST /assistant/voice    audio recorded on the phone -> {"heard", "answer"}
  POST /assistant/text     {"text"} -> {"heard", "answer"}
  POST /assistant/speak    {"text"} -> the answer spoken (WAV)
  POST /portal/done        the phone on the Pi's Wi-Fi is done with the sign-in page
  GET  /tv                 for the TV only (the "Controle Remoto" tile): QR codes that join the
                           Pi's Wi-Fi and open the remote. Served to the Pi itself only, so the
                           Wi-Fi password never leaves it.

HTTPS. Browsers only give the gyroscope and the microphone to HTTPS pages.
- On the tailnet: `tailscale serve` publishes the remote at https://<pi>.<tailnet>.ts.net:8446/.
- On the Pi's own Wi-Fi (modules/ap), without Tailscale: the access point's DNS points that same
  name to the Pi, and this program serves HTTPS on the access point's address, port 8446, with
  Tailscale's certificate for the name (copied by couchpie-ap-refresh). It reloads the
  certificate when renewed.

The sign-in prompt (captive portal). The access point's DNS sends the phones' internet checks
here (port 80 is redirected to this one). Until a phone taps "Pronto" on the page, its checks
are answered with a redirect to the remote, so the phone shows "sign in to the network", and
that opens the remote. After "Pronto" they're answered as the internet would, until the phone
leaves the Wi-Fi.

The voice assistant. The page records the phone's microphone; this program asks the assistant
through OpenAI-style endpoints: speech to text, the chat, text to speech. The defaults are PiPA's,
on the same Pi (pipa-assistant: pipa-stt and pipa-bridge). The section shows only when the chat
endpoint answers. Each phone gets its own conversation.

It answers only private addresses (the Pi's access point, the home network, the tailnet),
never the internet. Settings, all optional:
  PHONE_REMOTE_PORT            8096
  PHONE_REMOTE_HOME_URL        the "Casa" button's link; default: Home Assistant on the same
                               host, port 8123
  PHONE_REMOTE_AP_ADDRESS      the access point's address, for HTTPS and the sign-in page
                               (10.42.0.1)
  PHONE_REMOTE_AP_INTERFACE    its Wi-Fi interface (wlan0)
  PHONE_REMOTE_AP_CONNECTION   its NetworkManager connection, for the Wi-Fi's name (couchpie-ap)
  PHONE_REMOTE_AP_PASSWORD     the file with its password (~/.config/couchpie/ap-password)
  PHONE_REMOTE_TLS_PORT        8446
  PHONE_REMOTE_TLS_CERT/_KEY   /etc/couchpie/tls/phone.crt and .key
  PHONE_REMOTE_ASSISTANT       the assistant's name on the page (PiPA)
  PHONE_REMOTE_CHAT_URL        OpenAI-style chat API (http://127.0.0.1:8901/v1, PiPA's bridge;
                               empty = no assistant)
  PHONE_REMOTE_STT_URL         speech to text (http://127.0.0.1:8904/v1, pipa-stt)
  PHONE_REMOTE_TTS_URL         text to speech (the same; empty = the phone's own voice)
"""

